Supreme People's Court Supreme People's Procuratorate

Notice on Issuing the Opinions on Several Issues Concerning the Strict Application of Probation and Exemption from Criminal Punishment in Cases of Duty-related Crimes

Fa Fa [2012] No. 17

The Higher People's Courts and People's Procuratorates of all provinces, autonomous regions, and municipalities directly under the Central Government; the Military Court and Military Procuratorate of the People's Liberation Army; the Production and Construction Corps Branch of the Higher People's Court of Xinjiang Uygur Autonomous Region and the People's Procuratorate of Xinjiang Production and Construction Corps:

The Opinions on Several Issues Concerning the Strict Application of Probation and Exemption from Criminal Punishment in Cases of Duty-related Crimes, formulated by the Supreme People's Court and the Supreme People's Procuratorate, are hereby issued to you for earnest implementation.

August 8, 2012

Opinions on Several Issues Concerning the Strict Application of Probation and Exemption from Criminal Punishment in Cases of Duty-related Crimes

In order to further regulate the application of probation and exemption from criminal punishment in cases of duty-related crimes such as bribery, embezzlement, and malfeasance, and to ensure the legal and social effects of handling such cases, in accordance with the relevant provisions of the Criminal Law and in light of judicial practice, the following opinions are put forward regarding the specific application of probation and exemption from criminal punishment in cases of duty-related crimes:

1. Strictly control the application of probation and exemption from criminal punishment in cases of duty-related crimes. The application of criminal penalties in duty-related crime cases directly affects the actual effectiveness of anti-corruption efforts. The People's Courts and People's Procuratorates should deeply understand the serious social harm of duty-related crimes, correctly implement the criminal policy of combining leniency with severity, and fully leverage the punitive and preventive functions of criminal penalties. On the basis of a comprehensive grasp of the facts of the crime and sentencing circumstances, probation and exemption from criminal punishment should be applied strictly in accordance with the conditions stipulated in the Criminal Law. Both mitigating and aggravating circumstances should be considered; the penalty should be proportionate to the crime, and the method of executing the penalty should also be proportionate to the crime, effectively avoiding the negative impact of improper application of probation and exemption from criminal punishment.

2. Probation or exemption from criminal punishment shall generally not be applied to offenders of duty-related crimes under any of the following circumstances:

(1) Those who do not truthfully confess their crimes;

(2) Those who fail to return or surrender the illicit proceeds or use the illicit proceeds for illegal activities;

(3) Those who are principal offenders with serious circumstances in a joint crime;

(4) Those who have committed several duty-related crimes and are subject to cumulative punishment or punished as a single crime according to law;

(5) Those who have previously received administrative sanctions for duty-related disciplinary or illegal acts;

(6) The property involved in the crime consists of specific materials such as disaster relief, emergency rescue, flood control, special care, poverty alleviation, resettlement, relief, or epidemic prevention;

(7) Those who have the circumstance of soliciting bribes in the crime of bribery;

(8) Those who have the circumstance of engaging in malpractices for personal gain or abusing power with egregious circumstances in the crime of malfeasance;

(9) Other circumstances where probation or exemption from criminal punishment should not be applied.

3. For offenders of embezzlement or bribery who do not fall under the circumstances listed in Article 2 of this Opinion, who have fully returned or surrendered the illicit proceeds, and who are sentenced to fixed-term imprisonment of not more than three years according to law, if the conditions for applying probation stipulated in the Criminal Law are met, probation may be applied; if the provisions of Article 383, Paragraph 1, Item (3) of the Criminal Law are met and no criminal penalty is required according to law, exemption from criminal punishment may be applied.

For those who do not fall under the circumstances listed in Article 2 of this Opinion, who have misappropriated public funds for profit-making activities or have not repaid the funds for more than three months, thereby constituting a crime, and who have repaid the public funds before the pronouncement of the first-instance judgment, if they are sentenced to fixed-term imprisonment of not more than three years according to law and meet the conditions for applying probation stipulated in the Criminal Law, probation may be applied; if the funds were repaid before the incident and the circumstances are minor, such that no criminal penalty is required, exemption from criminal punishment may be applied.

4. When hearing cases of duty-related crimes, the People's Courts should pay attention to hearing the sentencing opinions of the procuratorial organs, the defendants, and the defense counsel, analyze the social reactions before and after the incident of influential cases, and may, when necessary, seek opinions from the organs that investigated the case. Probation or exemption from criminal punishment shall not be applied in cases of duty-related crimes with egregious circumstances and strong social reactions.

5. For cases where one of the circumstances listed in Article 2 of this Opinion is present, but based on the overall facts and sentencing circumstances, the procuratorial organ considers it truly necessary to apply probation or exemption from criminal punishment and accordingly proposes a sentencing recommendation, such recommendation shall be subject to discussion and decision by the Procuratorial Committee; if the trial court considers it truly necessary to apply probation or exemption from criminal punishment, such decision shall be subject to discussion and decision by the Adjudication Committee.
